The attached image (Figure 11) illustrates the histological comparison of injection sites two weeks after administration of aluminum hydroxide (AH) adjuvant (left) versus Nano-11 (right) with an antigen, stained with H&E. It demonstrates that Nano-11 causes significantly less residual inflammation at the injection site compared to AH. While AH induced extensive granulomatous inflammation characterized by large aggregates of macrophages and scattered eosinophils (*), Nano-11 resulted in much milder inflammation with relatively few macrophages arranged in slender cords. This supports the PDF's finding that Nano-11 induces a transient inflammatory response that resolves more quickly, contributing to its excellent safety profile as a vaccine adjuvant.